WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:13.794
Hi! Add a different little word to look, and the meaning changes completely! Today we'll learn phrasal verbs and idioms, which often appear in Primary 5 vocabulary questions.

2
00:00:13.944 --> 00:00:23.106
A phrasal verb is a verb plus a small word, like after, for or up. Look after means take care of: She looks after her baby brother.

3
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:27.282
Look for means search for: I am looking for my keys.

4
00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:32.610
Look up means find something in a dictionary: Look up the word in the dictionary.

5
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:39.210
Look forward to means wait happily for something: We look forward to the holidays.

6
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.714
Give up means stop trying: Don't give up!

7
00:00:42.864 --> 00:00:47.514
Put off means postpone: The match was put off because of the rain.

8
00:00:47.664 --> 00:00:51.906
Run out of means have none left: We have run out of milk.

9
00:00:52.056 --> 00:00:58.554
Break down means stop working, for a machine: The bus broke down on the way.

10
00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:08.514
An idiom's meaning can't be worked out word by word. A piece of cake is not about cake. It means very easy: The test was a piece of cake.

11
00:01:08.664 --> 00:01:13.962
Under the weather means feeling a little unwell: I'm feeling under the weather today.

12
00:01:14.112 --> 00:01:19.866
Once in a blue moon means very rarely: He eats fast food once in a blue moon.

13
00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:25.818
Let the cat out of the bag means to give away a secret by accident.

14
00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:31.242
When you meet a phrasal verb or idiom you don't know, look at the sentences around it.

15
00:01:31.392 --> 00:01:40.818
For example: Tom felt like giving up, but his coach said, carry on. The coach wanted him to keep going, so carry on means continue.

16
00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:48.258
Put your guess back into the sentence and read it. If it makes sense, you're probably right.

17
00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:56.730
Watch out! Some pupils think It's raining cats and dogs means animals are falling from the sky. Of course not!

18
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.682
Idioms can't be read word by word. This one means it is raining very heavily.

19
00:02:02.832 --> 00:02:07.314
So learn the meaning of an idiom as a whole.

20
00:02:07.464 --> 00:02:18.090
Your turn! We had to ___ the picnic because of the storm. Put off or look after? Pause and think.

21
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:26.346
The answer is put off. Because of the storm, the picnic was postponed. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:26.496 --> 00:02:36.402
Another one! The maths quiz was a piece of cake. What does that mean? Pause and think.

23
00:02:36.552 --> 00:02:42.522
It means the maths quiz was very easy. Did you get it right?

24
00:02:42.672 --> 00:02:52.578
Last one! The car ran ___ petrol on the highway. What goes in? Pause and think.

25
00:02:52.728 --> 00:02:59.826
The answer is out of. Run out of means have none left. Did you get it right?

26
00:02:59.976 --> 00:03:14.490
Remember: a phrasal verb is a verb plus a small word, and the meaning changes, like look after, look for, give up and put off. Learn idioms as a whole, never word by word, and use the context to guess.

27
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.554
Learn a new idiom today and share it with your family!

